### ## What's This?
Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) in PHP organizes code around objects -- instances of classes that bundle data (properties) and behavior (methods). Think of a class as a blueprint and an object as the actual thing built from that blueprint. OOP exists because procedural code becomes hard to maintain as applications grow; encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism help manage complexity by grouping related logic and hiding internal details. PHP's OOP model is class-based and borrows from Java and C++, supporting modern features like traits, interfaces, and anonymous classes.

### ## The Big Picture
Modern PHP relies heavily on OOP. Laravel, Symfony, and nearly every major framework are object-oriented. Composer packages are distributed as classes following PSR-4 autoloading. Understanding OOP is essential for working with frameworks, writing testable code, and contributing to the PHP ecosystem. Concepts like dependency injection, repositories, and service containers all build on OOP fundamentals.

### ## Core Ideas

### Classes and Objects
A <code>class</code> defines properties (variables) and methods (functions). An <code>object</code> is an instance created with <code>new</code>.
```php
class User {                          // class declaration defines a blueprint
    public string $name;              // property with public visibility
    public function greet(): string { // method that returns a string
        return "Hi, {$this->name}";  // $this refers to the current instance
    }
}
$user = new User();                   // instantiate a User object from the class
$user->name = "Alice";               // set property on the object
$message = $user->greet();            // call method, returns "Hi, Alice"
```

### Constructors and Property Promotion (PHP 8+)
<code>__construct</code> runs when an object is created. Constructor property promotion combines declaration and assignment in one place.
```php
class Product {
    public function __construct(      // constructor method runs at instantiation
        public string $name,          // PHP 8 promotes $name to a property automatically
        public float $price           // $price is also promoted automatically
    ) {}
}
$product = new Product("Widget", 9.99); // pass constructor arguments
```

### Visibility: public, protected, private
Visibility controls access to properties and methods. <code>public</code> is accessible everywhere. <code>protected</code> is accessible in the class and subclasses. <code>private</code> is accessible only within the defining class.
```php
class BankAccount {
    private float $balance = 0;       // only this class can modify $balance
    public function deposit(float $amount): void { // public method accessible anywhere
        if ($amount > 0) {            // guard against negative deposits
            $this->balance += $amount; // modify private property internally
        }
    }
    public function getBalance(): float { // public getter exposes balance safely
        return $this->balance;
    }
}
```

### Inheritance
A class can extend another class using <code>extends</code>, inheriting its public and protected methods and properties. Override parent methods by redefining them in the child.
```php
class Animal {
    public function speak(): string {  // base method to be overridden
        return "Some sound";
    }
}
class Dog extends Animal {            // Dog inherits from Animal
    public function speak(): string {  // overrides the parent speak method
        return "Woof";
    }
}
$dog = new Dog();
echo $dog->speak();                   // outputs "Woof" using the overridden method
```

### Abstract Classes and Interfaces
Abstract classes cannot be instantiated and may contain abstract methods that subclasses must implement. Interfaces declare method signatures without implementation; a class can implement multiple interfaces.
```php
interface Loggable {                   // contract defining required methods
    public function log(string $message): void; // signature only, no body
}
class FileLogger implements Loggable { // must implement all interface methods
    public function log(string $message): void {
        file_put_contents("log.txt", $message); // actual implementation
    }
}
```

### Traits
Traits are reusable code snippets that classes can import with <code>use</code>, solving PHP's single-inheritance limitation.
```php
trait Timestampable {                 // trait groups reusable behavior
    public function getCreatedAt(): string {
        return date("Y-m-d H:i:s");   // returns current timestamp as string
    }
}
class Post {
    use Timestampable;                 // imports all methods from the trait
}
$post = new Post();
echo $post->getCreatedAt();           // outputs current date and time
```

### Static Methods and Properties
Static members belong to the class itself, not to instances. Access them with <code>::</code> (Scope Resolution Operator).
```php
class MathHelper {
    public static function square(int $n): int { // static method on class level
        return $n * $n;
    }
}
echo MathHelper::square(5);           // calls static method without instantiation
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A polymorphic notification system using interfaces and inheritance:
```php
<?php
interface Notifiable {                 // contract for all notification types
    public function send(string $message): bool; // every notifier must implement send
}

class EmailNotifier implements Notifiable {
    public function send(string $message): bool {
        // mail($this->address, "Subject", $message); actual send logic
        return true;                   // pretend email was sent successfully
    }
}

class SmsNotifier implements Notifiable {
    public function send(string $message): bool {
        // sms_api::send($this->phone, $message); actual SMS logic
        return true;                   // pretend SMS was sent successfully
    }
}

class NotificationService {
    public function sendAll(array $notifiers, string $message): void {
        foreach ($notifiers as $notifier) { // iterate each notifier object
            if ($notifier instanceof Notifiable) { // type safety check via interface
                $notifier->send($message); // polymorphic call -- each class behaves differently
            }
        }
    }
}

$service = new NotificationService();
$service->sendAll(
    [new EmailNotifier(), new SmsNotifier()], // heterogeneous array of notifiers
    "System maintenance at midnight"    // message sent via each channel
);
?>
```
This demonstrates interfaces (contract), polymorphism (different send behaviors), type safety (instanceof check), and the foreach loop unifying heterogeneous notifiers.

### ## What's This?
Composer is the dependency manager for PHP. It handles installing, updating, and autoloading third-party packages so you don't have to manually download libraries or write complex require statements. Think of it as npm for JavaScript or pip for Python -- it reads a <code>composer.json</code> file describing your project's dependencies, resolves compatible versions, and downloads them into a <code>vendor/</code> directory. Composer exists because manually managing libraries and their versions across projects is error-prone and time-consuming; a standardized tool eliminates dependency hell.

### ## The Big Picture
Composer is the backbone of the modern PHP ecosystem. Every major framework (Laravel, Symfony, CakePHP) and thousands of libraries are distributed through Packagist, the default Composer repository. Composer's autoloader (PSR-4 and PSR-0) eliminates manual require/include calls. The lock file (<code>composer.lock</code>) ensures every developer and deployment uses identical versions. CI/CD pipelines run <code>composer install --no-dev</code> to exclude development tools from production.

### ## Core Ideas

### composer.json Structure
The <code>composer.json</code> file defines the project name, dependencies, autoloading rules, and scripts. The <code>require</code> section lists runtime packages; <code>require-dev</code> lists development-only packages.
```json
{
    "require": {
        "laravel/framework": "^10.0"  // any 10.x version compatible with semver
    },
    "require-dev": {
        "phpunit/phpunit": "^10.0"    // only needed during development/testing
    }
}
```

### Installing Packages
<code>composer install</code> reads <code>composer.lock</code> (or <code>composer.json</code> if no lock exists) and downloads exact versions. <code>composer require</code> adds a package and updates both files.
```bash
composer require guzzlehttp/guzzle    # adds Guzzle HTTP client to require section
composer require --dev phpstan/phpstan # adds PHPStan as a dev-only dependency
composer install                      # downloads all packages from lock file
```

### Version Constraints
Composer uses semantic versioning with constraints: <code>^</code> (compatible with major), <code>~</code> (patch-level changes only), <code>>=</code> (minimum version).
```json
{
    "require": {
        "monolog/monolog": "^2.0",     // >=2.0.0, <3.0.0 (allows minor/patch bumps)
        "php": ">=8.1",               // requires PHP 8.1 or higher
        "package/foo": "~2.3.0"       // >=2.3.0, <2.4.0 (only patch changes)
    }
}
```

### Autoloading (PSR-4)
The <code>autoload</code> section maps namespaces to directories. PSR-4 maps a namespace prefix to a directory, so <code>App\User</code> loads from <code>src/User.php</code>.
```json
{
    "autoload": {
        "psr-4": {
            "App\\": "src/"            // namespace App\ points to src/ directory
        }
    },
    "autoload-dev": {
        "psr-4": {
            "Tests\\": "tests/"         // namespace Tests\ points to tests/ directory
        }
    }
}
```
After defining, run <code>composer dump-autoload</code> to regenerate the autoloader files.

### Composer Scripts
Scripts run automatically at lifecycle events like <code>post-install-cmd</code> or <code>post-update-cmd</code>. Useful for clearing caches or running migrations after install.
```json
{
    "scripts": {
        "post-install-cmd": [          // runs after composer install completes
            "php artisan cache:clear"   // clears Laravel cache automatically
        ],
        "test": "phpunit"             // custom script: composer test runs phpunit
    }
}
```

### The Vendor Directory and Autoloader
After installation, packages live in <code>vendor/</code>. A single <code>require 'vendor/autoload.php'</code> in your entry point makes every class available without manual requires.
```php
require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php'; // loads Composer's autoloader

use GuzzleHttp\Client;                   // import class from installed package
$client = new Client();                   // instantiate without manual require
$response = $client->get('https://api.github.com'); // use package functionality
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A complete project setup with autoloading and a logging dependency:
```json
// composer.json
{
    "name": "acme/blog",
    "require": {
        "monolog/monolog": "^3.0"       // logging library constraint
    },
    "autoload": {
        "psr-4": {
            "Acme\\": "src/"            // all Acme classes under src/
        }
    },
    "scripts": {
        "post-install-cmd": [
            "@php -r 'echo \"Ready\";'" // placeholder post-install script
        ]
    }
}
```
```php
// src/Logger.php
namespace Acme;                          // namespace matches PSR-4 mapping

use Monolog\Level;                       // import Monolog severity levels
use Monolog\Handler\StreamHandler;       // import handler for file output

class Logger {
    private \Monolog\Logger $logger;    // property for Monolog instance

    public function __construct() {
        $this->logger = new \Monolog\Logger('app'); // create named logger
        $this->logger->pushHandler(      // attach handler to write logs
            new StreamHandler('app.log', Level::Debug) // write debug+ to file
        );
    }

    public function info(string $msg): void { // convenience wrapper method
        $this->logger->info($msg);        // delegate to Monolog's info method
    }
}
```
```php
// public/index.php
require __DIR__ . '/../vendor/autoload.php'; // single autoload include

use Acme\Logger;                        // our class autoloaded via PSR-4

$logger = new Logger();
$logger->info("Application started");   // writes to app.log via Monolog dependency

echo "Hello, world!";
```
This shows the full Composer workflow: defining dependencies and autoloading in composer.json, installing via CLI, and using both third-party and custom classes through the autoloader.

### ## What's This?
Laravel MVC is an architectural pattern that separates an application into three interconnected components: Models (data logic), Views (presentation), and Controllers (request handling). Laravel implements this pattern with a clean routing layer, Blade templating engine, and Eloquent ORM. MVC exists because mixing database queries, business logic, and HTML templates in a single file leads to unmaintainable spaghetti code -- separating concerns lets you change one part without breaking others. Laravel adds a service container, middleware, and Artisan CLI on top of the classic MVC pattern.

### ## The Big Picture
Laravel is the most popular PHP framework, and MVC is its architectural foundation. Every HTTP request follows a lifecycle: route -> middleware -> controller -> (model interaction) -> view response. Understanding this flow is essential for building Laravel applications. The framework extends MVC with features like dependency injection (service container), Eloquent for the model layer, and Blade for the view layer. Once you understand Laravel's MVC, you can navigate any Laravel project structure.

### ## Core Ideas

### Routing
Routes map HTTP methods and URLs to controller methods. They are defined in <code>routes/web.php</code> (browser routes with session state) or <code>routes/api.php</code> (stateless API routes).
```php
// routes/web.php
use App\Http\Controllers\PostController;

Route::get('/', function () {          // GET / returns a closure response
    return view('welcome');             // renders resources/views/welcome.blade.php
});

Route::get('/posts', [PostController::class, 'index']); // GET /posts calls PostController@index
Route::post('/posts', [PostController::class, 'store']); // POST /posts calls PostController@store
```

### Controllers
Controllers group related request-handling logic into classes. They receive request data, interact with models, and return responses. Artisan generates controllers with <code>php artisan make:controller</code>.
```php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\Post;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class PostController extends Controller {
    public function index() {          // handle GET /posts
        $posts = Post::all();           // fetch all posts from database via Eloquent
        return view('posts.index', compact('posts')); // render view with posts data
    }

    public function store(Request $request) { // handle POST /posts
        $post = Post::create(           // create a new Post record in database
            $request->validate([        // validate incoming request data
                'title' => 'required|max:255', // title must be present, max 255 chars
                'body' => 'required',    // body field is mandatory
            ])
        );
        return redirect('/posts/' . $post->id); // redirect to the new post's page
    }
}
```

### Blade Templates
Blade is Laravel's templating engine. It compiles to plain PHP and provides control structures, layout inheritance, and component syntax with <code>@</code> directives.
```php
<!-- resources/views/layouts/app.blade.php -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>@yield('title', 'Default')</title> <!-- section yield with default -->
</head>
<body>
    @yield('content')                    <!-- child templates fill this section -->
</body>
</html>
```
```php
<!-- resources/views/posts/index.blade.php -->
@extends('layouts.app')                  <!-- inherit from the app layout -->

@section('title', 'All Posts')           <!-- set the title section -->

@section('content')                      <!-- define content for the layout -->
    @foreach ($posts as $post)           <!-- Blade loop directive -->
        <h2>{{ $post->title }}</h2>      <!-- escaped output using double curly braces -->
        <p>{{ $post->excerpt }}</p>      <!-- display truncated post body -->
    @endforeach
@endsection                                  <!-- end the content section -->
```

### The Request Lifecycle
Every request flows through: <code>public/index.php</code> -> HTTP kernel -> middleware stack -> router -> controller -> response. The service container resolves dependencies automatically.
```php
// The entry point all requests hit
// public/index.php
$app = require __DIR__ . '/../bootstrap/app.php'; // bootstrap Laravel application
$kernel = $app->make(Kernel::class);    // resolve HTTP kernel from container
$response = $kernel->handle(            // process the incoming request
    Illuminate\Http\Request::capture()  // capture the HTTP request
)->send();                               // send response back to browser
$kernel->terminate($request, $response); // run any termination middleware
```

### Service Container
The service container manages class dependencies and performs dependency injection. You bind interfaces to implementations and the container resolves them automatically.
```php
// AppServiceProvider.php
use App\Contracts\PaymentGateway;
use App\Services\StripeGateway;

public function register(): void {
    $this->app->bind(PaymentGateway::class, StripeGateway::class); // bind interface to concrete
}

// Controller -- container injects the bound implementation automatically
public function __construct(
    private PaymentGateway $gateway    // container resolves StripeGateway here
) {}
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A blog feature with route, controller, model, and view:
```php
// routes/web.php
use App\Http\Controllers\PostController;

Route::resource('/posts', PostController::class); // generates all RESTful routes automatically
```
```php
// app/Models/Post.php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Post extends Model {               // Eloquent model for posts table
    protected $fillable = ['title', 'body']; // allow mass assignment for these fields
}
```
```php
// app/Http/Controllers/PostController.php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\Post;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class PostController extends Controller {
    public function index() {
        $posts = Post::latest()->get();  // fetch posts ordered by newest first
        return view('posts.index', compact('posts')); // pass posts to view
    }

    public function show(string $id) {
        $post = Post::findOrFail($id);   // find by ID or throw 404
        return view('posts.show', compact('post')); // render single post view
    }
}
```
```php
<!-- resources/views/posts/index.blade.php -->
@extends('layouts.app')

@section('content')
    <h1>Blog Posts</h1>
    @forelse ($posts as $post)           <!-- Blade: loop or run empty handler -->
        <article>
            <h2>{{ $post->title }}</h2>  <!-- escaped output of post title -->
            <p>{{ Str::limit($post->body, 150) }}</p> <!-- truncated body with 150 chars -->
            <a href="/posts/{{ $post->id }}">Read more</a>
        </article>
    @empty
        <p>No posts yet.</p>             <!-- shown when posts collection is empty -->
    @endforelse
@endsection
```
This demonstrates the full MVC cycle: a request hits a route, the controller fetches data from the model, passes it to a Blade view, and the view extends a layout to render the HTML response.

### ## What's This?
Eloquent is Laravel's built-in ORM (Object-Relational Mapper) that lets you interact with your database using PHP objects instead of writing raw SQL. Each database table has a corresponding Model class that wraps the table rows into objects with methods for querying, inserting, updating, and deleting. Migrations are version-controlled PHP files that define and modify your database schema programmatically. Eloquent exists because raw SQL scattered across code is hard to maintain, test, and refactor -- an ORM provides a clean, expressive API and prevents SQL injection through parameter binding.

### ## The Big Picture
Eloquent is the default ORM in Laravel and follows the Active Record pattern -- each model instance corresponds to a database row. Migrations allow teams to evolve the database schema collaboratively through version control. Eloquent relationships (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many) handle complex JOIN queries automatically. Combined with factories and seeders, Eloquent makes testing and prototyping fast. Understanding Eloquent is essential for any Laravel developer, as nearly every application involves database persistence.

### ## Core Ideas

### Migrations
Migrations are PHP classes in <code>database/migrations/</code> that define table schemas. The <code>up</code> method applies changes; <code>down</code> reverses them. Run <code>php artisan make:migration</code> to generate one.
```php
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

return new class extends Migration {
    public function up(): void {
        Schema::create('posts', function (Blueprint $table) { // create posts table
            $table->id();                // auto-incrementing primary key
            $table->string('title');     // VARCHAR column for title
            $table->text('body');        // TEXT column for body content
            $table->foreignId('user_id')->constrained(); // foreign key to users table
            $table->timestamps();        // created_at and updated_at columns
        });
    }

    public function down(): void {
        Schema::dropIfExists('posts');   // reverse: drop the posts table
    }
};
```

### Models
A model represents a database table. By convention, the <code>Post</code> model maps to the <code>posts</code> table. Models provide query methods, relationships, and accessors/mutators.
```php
namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Relations\BelongsTo;

class Post extends Model {
    protected $fillable = ['title', 'body', 'user_id']; // mass-assignable fields

    public function user(): BelongsTo {  // define inverse one-to-many relationship
        return $this->belongsTo(User::class); // each post belongs to one user
    }
}
```

### CRUD Operations
Eloquent provides methods for create, read, update, and delete without writing SQL. The query builder returns collections or model instances.
```php
$post = new Post();                      // create a new model instance
$post->title = "My First Post";          // set the title attribute
$post->body = "Hello world";            // set the body attribute
$post->save();                           // INSERT the record into the database

$posts = Post::where('user_id', 1)       // query builder: filter by user_id
              ->orderBy('created_at', 'desc') // order by newest first
              ->get();                   // execute query, return Collection of models

$post = Post::findOrFail(1);             // find by primary key or throw 404
$post->update(['title' => 'Updated']);   // UPDATE the title column

$post->delete();                         // DELETE the record from the database
```

### Relationships
Eloquent relationships define how models connect. <code>hasMany</code> / <code>belongsTo</code> handle one-to-many; <code>belongsToMany</code> handles many-to-many with pivot tables.
```php
class User extends Model {
    public function posts(): HasMany {   // a user has many posts
        return $this->hasMany(Post::class); // defines one-to-many relationship
    }

    public function roles(): BelongsToMany { // a user has many roles (many-to-many)
        return $this->belongsToMany(Role::class); // uses role_user pivot table
    }
}

// Usage: eager loading prevents N+1 query problem
$users = User::with('posts', 'roles')->get(); // eager loads both relationships
foreach ($users as $user) {              // iterate all users
    foreach ($user->posts as $post) {    // access related posts without extra queries
        echo $post->title;
    }
}
```

### Collections
Eloquent returns results as <code>Collection</code> objects, which provide map, filter, pluck, and other functional methods beyond plain arrays.
```php
$posts = Post::all();                    // fetch all posts as a Collection
$titles = $posts->pluck('title');        // extract all titles into a new Collection
$filtered = $posts->filter(fn($p) => strlen($p->title) > 10); // keep posts with long titles
$first = $posts->firstWhere('user_id', 1); // first post matching condition
```

### Accessors and Mutators
Accessors format attribute values when reading; mutators transform them when setting. Defined using <code>Attribute</code> return type in PHP 8+ style.
```php
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Casts\Attribute;

class Post extends Model {
    protected function excerpt(): Attribute { // define an accessor called "excerpt"
        return Attribute::make(
            get: fn() => substr($this->body, 0, 100) . '...', // truncate body to 100 chars
        );
    }
}

echo $post->excerpt;                     // accesses the computed attribute, not a column
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A complete blog query with filtering, relationship eager loading, and collection manipulation:
```php
<?php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Post;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class BlogController extends Controller {
    public function authorPosts(Request $request, string $userId) {
        $user = User::with('posts')       // eager load posts to avoid N+1 queries
                    ->findOrFail($userId); // find user or return 404

        $posts = $user->posts()           // query builder for user's posts
                    ->where('published', true) // only published posts
                    ->orderBy('created_at', 'desc') // newest first
                    ->get();              // execute and return Collection

        $summary = [                       // build structured response
            'author' => $user->name,       // user's display name
            'total' => $posts->count(),    // count of published posts
            'recent_titles' => $posts->take(5)->pluck('title'), // titles of 5 most recent
            'has_long_posts' => $posts->contains(fn($p) => strlen($p->body) > 1000), // any long posts?
        ];

        return response()->json($summary); // return JSON response
    }
}

// Example migration for the posts table
// database/migrations/xxxx_create_posts_table.php
use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

return new class extends Migration {
    public function up(): void {
        Schema::create('posts', function (Blueprint $table) {
            $table->id();
            $table->string('title', 255);
            $table->longText('body');
            $table->boolean('published')->default(false);
            $table->foreignId('user_id')->constrained()->cascadeOnDelete();
            $table->timestamps();
        });
    }

    public function down(): void {
        Schema::dropIfExists('posts');
    }
};
```
This ties migrations (schema definition), models (Post, User), relationships (belongsTo, hasMany), query builder (where, orderBy, get), eager loading (with), and collection methods (count, take, pluck, contains) into a single feature.

### ## What's This?
Middleware is a layer of code that sits between an incoming HTTP request and your application's response, running before the controller handles the request or after the response is generated. Think of it as an onion skin around your application -- each middleware can inspect, modify, or reject requests before they reach the core logic. Middleware exists because cross-cutting concerns like authentication, logging, CORS, and rate limiting should not be duplicated in every controller; middleware centralizes these concerns in reusable, composable layers.

### ## The Big Picture
Laravel's middleware system is a pipeline that processes requests through a stack of middleware classes before reaching the route handler. Global middleware runs on every request; route middleware applies to specific routes or groups. Authentication, CSRF protection, and request logging are all implemented as middleware. Laravel ships with built-in middleware for auth, guest, throttle, and more. Custom middleware can handle anything from request transformation to IP whitelisting. Authentication builds on middleware with guards (session, token, Sanctum) and authorization via Gates and Policies.

### ## Core Ideas

### Creating Middleware
Generate middleware with <code>php artisan make:middleware</code>. The <code>handle</code> method receives the request and a closure to pass control to the next middleware.
```php
namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Symfony\Component\HttpFoundation\Response;

class LogRequests {
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next): Response {
        \Log::info('Request: ' . $request->method() . ' ' . $request->path()); // log method and path
        $response = $next($request);     // pass request deeper into the pipeline
        \Log::info('Response status: ' . $response->status()); // log response status code
        return $response;               // return response back up the chain
    }
}
```

### Registering Middleware
Register middleware in <code>bootstrap/app.php</code> (Laravel 11+). Global middleware runs on every request; alias middleware is assigned to specific routes.
```php
// bootstrap/app.php (Laravel 11+)
use App\Http\Middleware\LogRequests;
use App\Http\Middleware\EnsureTokenIsValid;

return Application::configure(basePath: dirname(__DIR__))
    ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
        $middleware->append(LogRequests::class); // add to global middleware stack
        $middleware->alias([
            'token.valid' => EnsureTokenIsValid::class, // register alias for route use
        ]);
    })->create();

// Route file using alias middleware
Route::get('/admin', function () {      // protected route
    return view('admin.dashboard');
})->middleware('token.valid');           // only runs with valid token
```

### Authentication Guards
Guards define how users are authenticated per request. Laravel supports session-based, token-based, and Sanctum guards. The <code>auth</code> middleware protects routes.
```php
// config/auth.php (partial)
'guards' => [
    'web' => [                         // session-based guard for browser apps
        'driver' => 'session',
        'provider' => 'users',
    ],
    'api' => [                         // token-based guard for APIs
        'driver' => 'sanctum',          // uses Laravel Sanctum for API tokens
        'provider' => 'users',
    ],
],

// Route protection with auth middleware
Route::get('/dashboard', function () {  // protected route for logged-in users
    return view('dashboard');
})->middleware('auth');                  // redirects guests to login page

Route::get('/api/user', function (Request $request) {
    return $request->user();            // returns authenticated user via Sanctum
})->middleware('auth:sanctum');          // uses sanctum guard for API auth
```

### Gates and Policies
Gates are Closure-based authorization checks; Policies are classes organizing authorization logic around a model.
```php
// Define a gate in AppServiceProvider
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Gate;

Gate::define('edit-post', function (User $user, Post $post) {
    return $user->id === $post->user_id; // only the author can edit
});

// Using the gate in a controller
public function update(Request $request, Post $post) {
    Gate::authorize('edit-post', $post); // throws AuthorizationException if denied
    $post->update($request->only('title', 'body')); // safe to update after auth
}

// Policy alternative (auto-discovered for Post model)
class PostPolicy {
    public function update(User $user, Post $post): bool {
        return $user->id === $post->user_id; // same logic in policy form
    }
}
```

### Rate Limiting
Laravel's throttle middleware limits request frequency per IP or user, preventing abuse.
```php
// In route definition
Route::middleware('throttle:60,1')->group(function () { // 60 requests per minute max
    Route::get('/api/posts', [PostController::class, 'index']);
    Route::post('/api/posts', [PostController::class, 'store']);
});

// Named rate limiter in AppServiceProvider or bootstrap/app.php
RateLimiter::for('api', function (Request $request) {
    return Limit::perMinute(100)->by($request->user()?->id ?: $request->ip()); // 100/min per user or IP
});
```

### ## Wiring It Together
An API with logging, authentication, authorization, and rate limiting working together:
```php
// bootstrap/app.php (Laravel 11+)
use App\Http\Middleware\LogRequests;
use Illuminate\Cache\RateLimiting\Limit;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\RateLimiter;

return Application::configure(basePath: dirname(__DIR__))
    ->withMiddleware(function (Middleware $middleware) {
        $middleware->append(LogRequests::class); // global: log every request/response
        $middleware->alias([
            'check.role' => \App\Http\Middleware\CheckRole::class, // custom role check
        ]);
        $middleware->api(prepend: [     // prepend to 'api' middleware group
            \App\Http\Middleware\EnsureTokenIsValid::class,
        ]);
    })
    ->withRouting(
        api: __DIR__.'/../routes/api.php',
    );
```
```php
// routes/api.php
use App\Http\Controllers\PostController;

Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->group(function () {   // all routes require auth token
    Route::get('/posts', [PostController::class, 'index']);

    Route::post('/posts', [PostController::class, 'store'])
        ->middleware('throttle:30,1');                   // extra rate limit on writes

    Route::put('/posts/{post}', [PostController::class, 'update'])
        ->middleware('can:update,post');                 // authorize via PostPolicy
});
```
```php
// app/Http/Middleware/CheckRole.php
namespace App\Http\Middleware;

use Closure;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class CheckRole {
    public function handle(Request $request, Closure $next, string $role): mixed {
        if (!$request->user() || !$request->user()->hasRole($role)) {
            abort(403, 'Unauthorized');  // reject with 403 if wrong role
        }
        return $next($request);          // pass through if authorized
    }
}
```
```php
// app/Policies/PostPolicy.php
namespace App\Policies;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Post;

class PostPolicy {
    public function update(User $user, Post $post): bool {
        return $user->id === $post->user_id; // only post author can update
    }

    public function delete(User $user, Post $post): bool {
        return $user->id === $post->user_id || $user->isAdmin(); // author or admin
    }
}
```
This demonstrates middleware pipeline composition: logging (global), token validation (api group), auth:sanctum (route group), throttle (specific routes), and policy-based authorization (can middleware).

### ## What's This?
Testing in PHP means writing code that verifies your application behaves correctly. PHPUnit is the de facto standard testing framework, and Pest is a modern, expressive wrapper built on top of PHPUnit. Tests execute your application code with known inputs and assert that outputs match expectations. Testing exists because manual testing is slow, error-prone, and doesn't scale -- automated tests catch regressions, document behavior, and give you confidence to refactor. The testing pyramid recommends many fast unit tests, fewer integration tests, and even fewer end-to-end tests.

### ## The Big Picture
PHPUnit has been the backbone of PHP testing for decades. Pest offers a more readable syntax with functions like <code>it()</code>, <code>expect()</code>, and <code>toBe()</code>, while still running on PHPUnit under the hood. Laravel uses PHPUnit by default and provides helper methods for HTTP testing, database testing, and mocking. Factories generate test data, and seeders populate databases for integration tests. A solid test suite is non-negotiable for production applications -- it enables continuous delivery, documents edge cases, and prevents regressions during refactoring.

### ## Core Ideas

### PHPUnit Test Structure
Tests are classes extending <code>PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase</code>. Methods prefixed with <code>test</code> or annotated with <code>#[Test]</code> are test cases. Assertions compare actual vs. expected values.
```php
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class UserTest extends TestCase {
    public function test_full_name_is_constructed_from_first_and_last(): void {
        $user = new \App\User('John', 'Doe'); // create a user instance
        $this->assertEquals('John Doe', $user->fullName()); // assert fullName matches
    }

    #[Test]
    public function email_validation_rejects_invalid_formats(): void {
        $validator = new \App\Validator();
        $this->assertFalse($validator->isEmail('not-an-email')); // assert returns false
    }
}
```

### Pest Syntax
Pest uses functions instead of classes. The <code>test()</code> and <code>it()</code> functions define cases; <code>expect()</code> enables a fluent assertion API.
```php
// tests/Unit/UserTest.php
test('full name combines first and last', function () {
    $user = new \App\User('Jane', 'Doe');
    expect($user->fullName())->toBe('Jane Doe'); // fluent assertion
});

it('validates email format correctly', function () {
    $validator = new \App\Validator();
    expect($validator->isEmail('jane@example.com'))->toBeTrue(); // passes
    expect($validator->isEmail('bad'))->toBeFalse(); // fails for bad input
});
```

### Data Providers
Data providers supply multiple input sets to a single test, avoiding duplication.
```php
use PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\DataProvider;

class MathTest extends TestCase {
    #[DataProvider('additionProvider')]
    public function test_add(int $a, int $b, int $expected): void {
        $this->assertEquals($expected, \App\Math::add($a, $b));
    }

    public static function additionProvider(): array {
        return [
            [1, 2, 3],                 // test case: 1 + 2 = 3
            [0, 0, 0],                 // test case: 0 + 0 = 0
            [-1, 1, 0],               // test case: -1 + 1 = 0
        ];
    }
}

// Pest equivalent
it('adds numbers correctly', function (int $a, int $b, int $expected) {
    expect(\App\Math::add($a, $b))->toBe($expected);
})->with([
    [1, 2, 3],
    [0, 0, 0],
    [-1, 1, 0],
]);
```

### Mocking
Mocks replace real objects with test doubles that simulate behavior and verify interactions.
```php
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class OrderServiceTest extends TestCase {
    public function test_order_sends_notification(): void {
        $mailer = $this->createMock(\App\Mailer::class); // create test double
        $mailer->expects($this->once())  // assert method is called exactly once
               ->method('send')          // specify the method to mock
               ->with('Thank you!')      // assert argument passed to send
               ->willReturn(true);       // define return value

        $service = new \App\OrderService($mailer); // inject mock
        $service->processOrder(100);     // execute with mocked dependency
    }
}
```

### Laravel HTTP Tests
Laravel provides methods like <code>get()</code>, <code>post()</code>, and <code>assertStatus()</code> to test HTTP endpoints without a real server.
```php
namespace Tests\Feature;

use Tests\TestCase;
use App\Models\Post;
use App\Models\User;

class PostTest extends TestCase {
    public function test_authenticated_user_can_create_post(): void {
        $user = User::factory()->create(); // create a test user via factory

        $response = $this->actingAs($user) // authenticate as this user
                         ->post('/posts', [ // send POST request to /posts
                             'title' => 'Test Title',
                             'body' => 'Test body content',
                         ]);

        $response->assertStatus(302);      // assert redirect after creation
        $this->assertDatabaseHas('posts', [ // verify database has the record
            'title' => 'Test Title',
        ]);
    }
}
```

### SetUp and TearDown
Run common setup before each test and cleanup after. Prevents duplication of test scaffolding.
```php
use PHPUnit\Framework\TestCase;

class UserRepositoryTest extends TestCase {
    private \App\UserRepository $repository;

    protected function setUp(): void {    // runs before every test method
        $this->repository = new \App\UserRepository(); // fresh instance per test
    }

    protected function tearDown(): void {  // runs after every test method
        \Mockery::close();                 // clean up Mockery expectations
    }

    public function test_find_returns_null_for_missing_user(): void {
        $result = $this->repository->find(999); // non-existent ID
        $this->assertNull($result);         // expect null result
    }
}
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A full Laravel feature test for a blog API endpoint:
```php
<?php
namespace Tests\Feature;

use Tests\TestCase;
use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Post;
use Illuminate\Foundation\Testing\RefreshDatabase;

class PostApiTest extends TestCase {
    use RefreshDatabase;                  // reset database after each test

    public function test_unauthenticated_user_cannot_create_post(): void {
        $response = $this->postJson('/api/posts', [ // POST as JSON without auth
            'title' => 'Hacked Post',
            'body' => 'Should not appear',
        ]);

        $response->assertStatus(401);      // assert unauthorized response
    }

    public function test_authenticated_user_can_list_own_posts(): void {
        $user = User::factory()            // use factory to create a test user
            ->has(Post::factory()->count(3)) // create 3 posts for this user
            ->create();

        $response = $this->actingAs($user) // authenticate
                         ->getJson('/api/posts'); // GET request expecting JSON

        $response->assertStatus(200)       // assert successful response
                 ->assertJsonCount(3);     // assert response has 3 posts
    }

    #[\PHPUnit\Framework\Attributes\DataProvider('validationProvider')]
    public function test_post_validation_rules(string $field, mixed $value, string $errorKey): void {
        $user = User::factory()->create();

        $response = $this->actingAs($user)
                         ->postJson('/api/posts', [
                             $field => $value,
                         ]);

        $response->assertStatus(422)       // assert validation error status
                 ->assertJsonValidationErrors([$errorKey]); // check specific field error
    }

    public static function validationProvider(): array {
        return [
            'title required' => ['title', '', 'title'],
            'title too long' => ['title', str_repeat('a', 256), 'title'],
            'body required' => ['body', null, 'body'],
        ];
    }
}
```
This combines PHPUnit structure, RefreshDatabase trait, Laravel HTTP test helpers (actingAs, postJson, getJson), factories with relationships, JSON assertions, and a data provider for comprehensive validation coverage.

### ## What's This?
WordPress plugin and theme development is the practice of extending WordPress's functionality without modifying core files. A plugin adds custom features, while a theme controls the visual presentation. The WordPress plugin system is built on hooks -- actions (trigger points) and filters (data modifiers) -- that let your code run at specific moments during request execution. Plugin and theme development exists because directly editing WordPress core would lose changes on every update; hooks provide stable, upgrade-safe extension points that survive core upgrades.

### ## The Big Picture
WordPress powers roughly 40% of all websites. Its plugin architecture has created a massive ecosystem with over 60,000 free plugins. Themes use a template hierarchy that falls back from page-specific templates to <code>index.php</code>. Custom post types extend WordPress beyond blog posts into any content type (products, events, portfolios). The Loop is the central pattern for displaying content. Understanding hooks (actions and filters), the template hierarchy, the Loop, and enqueuing assets is essential for any WordPress developer building custom websites or contributing to the ecosystem.

### ## Core Ideas

### Plugin Structure
A plugin is a PHP file with a specific header comment placed in <code>wp-content/plugins/</code>. The header identifies the plugin in the admin dashboard.
```php
<?php
/**
 * Plugin Name: Custom Quotes      // displayed name in plugins list
 * Description: Adds a custom quote post type and shortcode
 * Version: 1.0.0
 * Author: Your Name
 */

// Prevent direct access
if (!defined('ABSPATH')) {           // ABSPATH is WordPress root directory constant
    exit;                            // stop execution if accessed directly
}
```

### Actions and Filters
Actions run at specific points (e.g., <code>init</code>, <code>wp_head</code>); filters modify data (e.g., <code>the_content</code>, <code>excerpt_length</code>). Both use <code>add_action</code> and <code>add_filter</code>.
```php
// Action: runs code at a specific execution point
add_action('init', 'register_my_post_type'); // hook function into WordPress init event

function register_my_post_type(): void {
    register_post_type('quote', [       // register a custom post type
        'public' => true,
        'label' => 'Quotes',
        'supports' => ['title', 'editor', 'thumbnail'],
    ]);
}

// Filter: modifies content before output
add_filter('the_content', 'add_quote_prefix'); // filter post content before display

function add_quote_prefix(string $content): string {
    if (get_post_type() === 'quote') {  // only modify quote post type
        return '<blockquote>' . $content . '</blockquote>'; // wrap in blockquote tags
    }
    return $content;                    // pass through unmodified
}
```

### Shortcodes
Shortcodes are macros that expand to dynamic content. Register them with <code>add_shortcode</code>. The function returns a string that replaces the shortcode in post content.
```php
add_shortcode('random_quote', 'render_random_quote'); // register [random_quote] shortcode

function render_random_quote(): string {
    $args = [
        'post_type' => 'quote',        // query the quote post type
        'posts_per_page' => 1,          // only fetch one post
        'orderby' => 'rand',            // randomize the order
    ];
    $query = new WP_Query($args);       // create custom database query

    if ($query->have_posts()) {         // check if any posts were found
        $query->the_post();             // set up post data for template tags
        return '<p class="quote">' . get_the_content() . '</p>'; // return post content
    }
    return '<p>No quotes found.</p>';   // fallback when no posts exist
}

// Usage in post editor: [random_quote]
```

### The Loop
The Loop is WordPress's standard way of displaying posts. It iterates through posts from a query, setting up global post data for each iteration.
```php
// Typically in index.php or archive.php
if (have_posts()) {                     // check if query returned any posts
    while (have_posts()) {              // loop while there are posts to process
        the_post();                     // set up $post global with current post data
        ?>
        <article>
            <h2><a href="<?php the_permalink(); ?>"><?php the_title(); ?></a></h2>
            <div><?php the_excerpt(); ?></div>
        </article>
        <?php
    }
} else {
    echo '<p>No posts found.</p>';      // shown when no posts match query
}
```

### Enqueuing Assets
Use <code>wp_enqueue_script</code> and <code>wp_enqueue_style</code> to load JavaScript and CSS. Always enqueue rather than hardcode tags to avoid duplication and manage dependencies.
```php
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', 'load_plugin_assets'); // hook into frontend asset loading

function load_plugin_assets(): void {
    wp_enqueue_style(                  // register and enqueue a stylesheet
        'my-plugin-styles',            // unique handle for this stylesheet
        plugin_dir_url(__FILE__) . 'assets/style.css', // full URL to CSS file
        [],                            // no dependencies
        '1.0.0'                        // version string for cache busting
    );

    wp_enqueue_script(                 // register and enqueue a JavaScript file
        'my-plugin-script',            // unique handle
        plugin_dir_url(__FILE__) . 'assets/script.js', // full URL to JS file
        ['jquery'],                    // depends on jQuery being loaded first
        '1.0.0',                       // version
        true                           // load in footer (not header)
    );
}
```

### Theme Template Hierarchy
WordPress selects the appropriate template file based on the query. The hierarchy falls back from most specific to most generic (<code>index.php</code>).
```php
// templates in increasing specificity:
// index.php          -> ultimate fallback for all requests
// archive.php         -> archive pages (category, tag, date, author)
// single.php          -> single post of any post type
// single-quote.php    -> single post of "quote" post type only
// page.php            -> static pages
// page-about.php      -> static page with slug "about"
// front-page.php      -> site front page (overrides index.php)

// Example single-quote.php
<?php
get_header();                           // load header.php
if (have_posts()) {
    while (have_posts()) {
        the_post();
        ?>
        <article>
            <blockquote><?php the_content(); ?></blockquote>
            <cite>&mdash; <?php the_title(); ?></cite>
        </article>
        <?php
    }
}
get_footer();                           // load footer.php
```

### ## Wiring It Together
A complete plugin that creates a "Testimonial" custom post type with a shortcode and proper asset enqueuing:
```php
<?php
/**
 * Plugin Name: Testimonials Manager
 * Description: Custom testimonials post type with shortcode display
 * Version: 1.0.0
 */

if (!defined('ABSPATH')) exit;         // block direct file access

// 1. Register custom post type on WordPress init
add_action('init', 'tm_register_post_type');

function tm_register_post_type(): void {
    register_post_type('testimonial', [  // register testimonial CPT
        'public' => true,
        'label' => 'Testimonials',
        'supports' => ['title', 'editor', 'thumbnail'],
        'menu_icon' => 'dashicons-format-quote',
    ]);
}

// 2. Add a shortcode to display testimonials
add_shortcode('testimonials', 'tm_render_testimonials');

function tm_render_testimonials(): string {
    $query = new WP_Query([
        'post_type' => 'testimonial',
        'posts_per_page' => 5,
        'orderby' => 'date',
        'order' => 'DESC',
    ]);

    if (!$query->have_posts()) {
        return '<p>No testimonials yet.</p>';
    }

    $output = '<div class="testimonials-list">';
    while ($query->have_posts()) {
        $query->the_post();
        $output .= '<blockquote>';
        $output .= get_the_content();
        $output .= '<cite>&mdash; ' . get_the_title() . '</cite>';
        $output .= '</blockquote>';
    }
    $output .= '</div>';
    wp_reset_postdata();                 // restore global post data
    return $output;
}

// 3. Enqueue stylesheet
add_action('wp_enqueue_scripts', 'tm_enqueue_assets');

function tm_enqueue_assets(): void {
    wp_enqueue_style(
        'tm-styles',
        plugin_dir_url(__FILE__) . 'testimonials.css',
        [],
        '1.0.0'
    );
}
```
This plugin demonstrates the full cycle: registering a custom post type via an action hook, creating a shortcode with WP_Query and The Loop, and enqueuing frontend assets safely.
